## Explanation
To check if a number is even, we need to determine if it can be divided by 2 without leaving anything left over. Think of this like sorting items into pairs: if you have a pile of socks and you try to pair them all up, an even number means every sock has a match and nothing is left behind. In C, we use the **Modulus operator (`%`)**, which calculates the remainder of a division. If a number divided by 2 has a remainder of 0, the number is even.

## Use Cases
This logic is used whenever a program needs to categorize data or perform repeating actions. For example, you might use it to apply a different color to every other row in a table, or to trigger an event only on every second button click. It is a fundamental way to teach a computer how to recognize patterns in numbers.

